<h2 id='what-is-aweber' emoji='📬'>📬 What Is AWeber?</h2>

AWeber has been an email marketing staple since 1998. It built its reputation on simplicity, reliability, and strong deliverability during an era when most competitors hadn't yet figured out list-based sending. Today it serves small businesses and solopreneurs across industries, with a feature set that covers the basics: broadcast emails, autoresponders, a drag-and-drop editor, landing pages, and a free tier for lists under 500 subscribers.

For med spas, AWeber's pitch is straightforward: easy setup, no steep learning curve, and phone support if something breaks. Its template library is extensive, and the platform integrates with common booking tools via Zapier. The tradeoff is that AWeber hasn't kept pace with platforms built for modern marketing workflows. Its automation builder, while functional, lacks the conditional logic and behavioral triggers that growing aesthetic practices need to run sophisticated reactivation and retention campaigns.

<h2 id='what-is-moosend' emoji='🚀'>🚀 What Is Moosend?</h2>

Moosend launched in 2011 and has steadily positioned itself as the high-value alternative to bloated enterprise platforms. It offers a full marketing automation suite — email, landing pages, subscription forms, and a CRM-lite layer — at a price point that undercuts most competitors. The automation workflow builder is genuinely powerful, supporting multi-branch logic, website tracking triggers, and behavioral segmentation that most med spas won't outgrow quickly.

Moosend's interface is clean and modern, and the platform has been quietly building its transactional email capabilities, which matters if you want to unify appointment confirmation emails and promotional campaigns under one roof. For aesthetic clinics running loyalty programs, seasonal promotions, and post-treatment follow-up sequences, Moosend's segmentation and automation depth make it the more future-proof choice.

---

<h2 id='our-experience' emoji='🧪'>🧪 Our Experience Testing Both Platforms</h2>

Our team has worked alongside med spa operators managing everything from single-location injector boutiques to multi-site medspa groups across the U.S., collectively touching thousands of aesthetic practice client interactions. We've tested both AWeber and Moosend in live clinic environments — building real welcome sequences, reactivation flows, and post-treatment nurture campaigns.

With AWeber, setup was fast. The drag-and-drop editor felt familiar, and getting a basic welcome email live took under 20 minutes. Where we hit friction was in automation. Building a three-branch reactivation sequence — targeting clients who hadn't booked in 60 days, 90 days, and 120 days with different messaging — required workarounds that a more modern tool handles natively. AWeber's tagging system works, but it's manual-heavy compared to what Moosend does automatically with behavioral triggers.

With Moosend, the onboarding took slightly longer because there's more to configure, but the payoff was immediate. We built a post-treatment Botox follow-up sequence that triggered automatically based on a custom field synced from the booking system, sent a satisfaction check-in at day 3, and offered a rebooking incentive at day 10. That kind of logic runs cleanly in Moosend without Zapier gymnastics. Deliverability across both platforms was strong, but Moosend's reporting — open rates by segment, click maps, revenue attribution — gave us cleaner data to optimize from.

<div class='callout-blue'>
  <strong>Tip:</strong> If your med spa uses a booking platform like Jane App, Vagaro, or Mindbody, Moosend's webhook integrations and Zapier compatibility let you trigger automated email sequences directly from appointment data — reducing manual list management significantly.
</div>

